//! image/click_between.rye -- the THREE-CLICK BETWEENNESS relation: turn THREE points into THREE whole-group selections -- two ENDPOINT taps and
//! one MIDDLE-candidate tap -- then read whether the middle group sits BETWEEN the two endpoint groups, and read it TWO ways at once: in the reading
//! SEQUENCE (does the middle's page rank fall between the endpoints') and in SPACE (does the middle's group frame lie inside the box the two endpoint
//! frames span). Where image/click_nearest.rye reads which of two candidates is NEARER an anchor, this rung reads whether a candidate is BETWEEN two
//! others -- the ternary companion the near read set up. Exact integers, NO float and NO tolerance.
//!
//! Season G (Open Media Primitives) -- image/hit_test.rye names the ONE object under a point; image/overlap_clusters.rye's `cluster_of` names the
//! connected group; image/click_order.rye's `click_group_min_rank` reads a tapped group's page position in the total top-to-bottom, left-to-right
//! reading order; image/click_bounds.rye's `click_bbox` reads a tapped group's handle FRAME. This rung LIFTS the arity one step past image/
//! click_nearest.rye -- from "which of two is nearer that one" to "is this one between those two" -- and reads betweenness in the two registers the
//! click family already speaks: the reading register (image/click_order.rye) and the spatial register (image/click_bounds.rye). A reading over
//! readings: no plane of its own, no pixel written, no rank re-sorted, no box re-measured.
//!
//! The DECISIVE crux: reading-order betweenness and spatial betweenness are DIFFERENT relations -- a group can sit between two others in the reading
//! sequence yet NOT in space, or sit between them in space yet read OUTSIDE them on the page. And the two relations differ in KIND: reading order is
//! a STRICT TOTAL order, so for any three distinct-rank groups EXACTLY ONE is the reading-middle, while spatial betweenness is only a PARTIAL relation
//! over the plane, so a triple can have NO spatial middle at all. It is the box-is-a-prefilter lesson lifted to a TRIPLE: a bounding-box span
//! OVER-CLAIMS the "between" the reading order names exactly. Shapes over six singleton objects on a 20x20 canvas -- P block box (1,1)-(2,2) rank 1,
//! V a TALL block box (11,1)-(12,12) rank 2, W block box (18,1)-(19,2) rank 3, Q block box (4,4)-(5,5) rank 4, S block box (15,4)-(16,5) rank 5, R
//! block box (7,7)-(8,8) rank 6 -- no boxes overlapping, so six groups:
//!
//!   BOTH -- P, Q, R on the diagonal: Q reads between P (rank 1) and R (rank 6) AND Q's box (4,4)-(5,5) sits inside the P..R span box (1,1)-(8,8).
//!   `classify_betweenness` reads `both`.
//!
//!   ORDER_ONLY -- P, V, R: V reads between P (rank 1) and R (rank 6) since V's rank 2 falls in (1,6), yet V's box (11,1)-(12,12) lies OUTSIDE the
//!   P..R span box (1,1)-(8,8) on x. Between on the page, not in space -- `order_only`.
//!
//!   SPACE_ONLY -- V, S, W: S's box (15,4)-(16,5) sits inside the V..W span box (11,1)-(19,12), yet S reads AFTER both V (rank 2) and W (rank 3) since
//!   its rank 5 falls OUTSIDE (2,3). Between in space, not on the page -- `space_only`. The tall endpoint V stretches the span box down over S while the
//!   page order, keyed on the top edge, seats S well after both endpoints.
//!
//!   NEITHER -- P, R, Q: R reads after both P (rank 1) and Q (rank 4) and R's box (7,7)-(8,8) lies outside the P..Q span box (1,1)-(5,5). Between in
//!   neither register -- `neither`.
//!
//! And the two relations part in KIND, not only in verdict. TOTALITY OF THE READING MIDDLE -- over any three distinct groups exactly one is the
//! reading-between: of P, Q, R only Q reads between the other two. NO SPATIAL MIDDLE AT ALL -- over Q, R, S none sits inside the span of the other two
//! (Q, R, and S form an L, so each falls outside the box the other pair spans), yet the reading order still names S the middle of the three. The
//! total order always seats a middle; the plane need not.
//!
//! Compose not braid. This rung OWNS only the ternary betweenness tests over the two registers. image/click_order.rye owns `click_group_min_rank`
//! (the group's page rank) and `click_group_id` (the click-to-group id for the endpoint-distinctness check); image/click_bounds.rye owns `click_bbox`
//! (the group's handle frame); image/hit_test.rye owns the point-to-object arbiter; image/overlap_clusters.rye owns `cluster_of`. No rank re-sorted,
//! no matrix recomputed, no pixel re-read.
//!
//! Everything is bounded before a byte: each click resolves to at most one label through the hit test, each rank read walks the object count bounded
//! by image/scene_graph.rye's cap, each frame read the group length bounded by image/label.rye's `label_max_pixels`. A betweenness needs two DISTINCT
//! endpoint groups, so two endpoint taps sharing a group refuse `SameGroup` by name (there is no interval to be between); a tap on background refuses
//! `EmptyClick`; a point off the map refuses `PointOutOfBounds`, having read nothing. No network, no key, no funds -- the "is this one between those
//! two" a surface names the day a keeper points at three things.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run image/click_between.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;
const label = @import("label.rye");
const qoi = @import("qoi.rye");
const scene_manifest = @import("scene_manifest.rye");
const scene_graph = @import("scene_graph.rye");
const hit_test = @import("hit_test.rye");
const overlap_clusters = @import("overlap_clusters.rye");
const click_order = @import("click_order.rye");
const click_bounds = @import("click_bounds.rye");
const selection_bounds = @import("selection_bounds.rye");

pub const click_between_version = "20260819.070130";

pub const ClickBetweenError = error{
    SameGroup,
};

/// How a middle-candidate group sits between two endpoint groups, read in both registers at once -- the reading SEQUENCE and SPACE. The four cases
/// are exhaustive and name exactly where the two betweenness relations agree and disagree.
pub const Betweenness = enum {
    both, // the middle group reads between the endpoints AND its frame lies inside their span box
    order_only, // between in the reading sequence, yet its frame lies outside the endpoints' span box
    space_only, // its frame lies inside the endpoints' span box, yet it reads outside them on the page
    neither, // between in neither register
};

/// Guard that the two ENDPOINT taps land in DISTINCT groups -- a betweenness needs a real interval, so two endpoints in one group refuse `SameGroup`.
/// Resolving each tap to its group first means a MISS refuses `EmptyClick` and an off-map point refuses `PointOutOfBounds` before the same-group test.
fn assert_distinct_endpoints(clustering: overlap_clusters.Clustering, map: *const label.LabelMap, ax: u32, ay: u32, bx: u32, by: u32) !void {
    // invariant: the clustering and the map describe the same scene.
    assert(clustering.components == map.count);

    const ga = try click_order.click_group_id(clustering, map, ax, ay);
    const gb = try click_order.click_group_id(clustering, map, bx, by);
    // invariant: each endpoint resolved to a real dense group id.
    assert(ga >= 1);
    assert(ga <= clustering.clusters);
    assert(gb >= 1);
    assert(gb <= clustering.clusters);
    if (ga == gb) return error.SameGroup;
}

/// TRUE when the MIDDLE tap's group reads BETWEEN the two ENDPOINT taps' groups in the total top-to-bottom, left-to-right page order -- its
/// `click_group_min_rank` falls strictly inside the open interval between the two endpoint ranks, in either endpoint order. Reading rank is a STRICT
/// TOTAL order, so this is a clean one-dimensional betweenness. Refuses `SameGroup` when the two endpoints share a group (no interval), `EmptyClick`
/// on any miss, `PointOutOfBounds` off the map.
pub fn between_in_order(clustering: overlap_clusters.Clustering, graph: scene_graph.SceneGraph, map: *const label.LabelMap, ax: u32, ay: u32, mx: u32, my: u32, bx: u32, by: u32) !bool {
    // invariant: the clustering, the graph, and the map describe the same scene.
    assert(clustering.components == graph.components);
    assert(clustering.components == map.count);

    try assert_distinct_endpoints(clustering, map, ax, ay, bx, by);
    const rank_a = try click_order.click_group_min_rank(clustering, graph, map, ax, ay);
    const rank_b = try click_order.click_group_min_rank(clustering, graph, map, bx, by);
    const rank_m = try click_order.click_group_min_rank(clustering, graph, map, mx, my);
    // invariant: distinct endpoint groups hold distinct page ranks (a strict total order).
    assert(rank_a != rank_b);
    const lo = @min(rank_a, rank_b);
    const hi = @max(rank_a, rank_b);
    // invariant: the interval is a real open span of ranks.
    assert(lo < hi);
    return lo < rank_m and rank_m < hi;
}

/// TRUE when the MIDDLE tap's group frame sits WHOLLY inside the box the two ENDPOINT taps' group frames SPAN -- the axis-aligned rectangle whose
/// corners are the min and max of the two endpoint frames. A bounding-box betweenness: the span box always holds both endpoint frames, and this asks
/// whether it holds the middle's too. Exact integer box math, no float. Refuses `SameGroup` when the endpoints share a group, `EmptyClick` on any
/// miss, `PointOutOfBounds` off the map.
pub fn between_in_space(garden: std.mem.Allocator, clustering: overlap_clusters.Clustering, graph: scene_graph.SceneGraph, manifest: scene_manifest.Manifest, map: *const label.LabelMap, ax: u32, ay: u32, mx: u32, my: u32, bx: u32, by: u32) !bool {
    // invariant: the clustering, the graph, the manifest, and the map describe the same scene.
    assert(clustering.components == graph.components);
    assert(graph.components == manifest.components);
    assert(manifest.components == map.count);

    try assert_distinct_endpoints(clustering, map, ax, ay, bx, by);
    const box_a = try click_bounds.click_bbox(garden, graph, manifest, map, ax, ay);
    const box_b = try click_bounds.click_bbox(garden, graph, manifest, map, bx, by);
    const box_m = try click_bounds.click_bbox(garden, graph, manifest, map, mx, my);

    // the span box unions the two endpoint frames -- the corners are the extremes of the two, so it holds both endpoints by construction.
    const span_min_x = @min(box_a.min_x, box_b.min_x);
    const span_min_y = @min(box_a.min_y, box_b.min_y);
    const span_max_x = @max(box_a.max_x, box_b.max_x);
    const span_max_y = @max(box_a.max_y, box_b.max_y);
    // invariant: the span box is well-formed on both axes.
    assert(span_max_x >= span_min_x);
    assert(span_max_y >= span_min_y);
    // invariant: the span box holds each endpoint frame whole -- betweenness of the endpoints is trivially true.
    assert(span_min_x <= box_a.min_x);
    assert(box_a.max_x <= span_max_x);
    assert(span_min_x <= box_b.min_x);
    assert(box_b.max_x <= span_max_x);

    return span_min_x <= box_m.min_x and box_m.max_x <= span_max_x and span_min_y <= box_m.min_y and box_m.max_y <= span_max_y;
}

/// The full betweenness verdict of a middle candidate against two endpoints -- read both registers and name where they agree and disagree. `both`
/// when the middle reads between the endpoints and its frame lies inside their span box, `order_only` when it reads between yet its frame escapes the
/// box, `space_only` when its frame lies inside yet it reads outside, `neither` when both say no. Refuses `SameGroup` when the endpoints share a
/// group, `EmptyClick` on any miss, `PointOutOfBounds` off the map.
pub fn classify_betweenness(garden: std.mem.Allocator, clustering: overlap_clusters.Clustering, graph: scene_graph.SceneGraph, manifest: scene_manifest.Manifest, map: *const label.LabelMap, ax: u32, ay: u32, mx: u32, my: u32, bx: u32, by: u32) !Betweenness {
    // invariant: the clustering, the graph, the manifest, and the map describe the same scene.
    assert(clustering.components == graph.components);
    assert(graph.components == manifest.components);
    assert(manifest.components == map.count);

    const in_order = try between_in_order(clustering, graph, map, ax, ay, mx, my, bx, by);
    const in_space = try between_in_space(garden, clustering, graph, manifest, map, ax, ay, mx, my, bx, by);
    if (in_order and in_space) return .both;
    if (in_order) return .order_only;
    if (in_space) return .space_only;
    return .neither;
}

// --- selftest: the DECISIVE space-vs-order betweenness disagreement over SIX singleton objects on a 20x20 canvas -- P box (1,1)-(2,2) rank 1, V a
//     TALL block box (11,1)-(12,12) rank 2, W box (18,1)-(19,2) rank 3, Q box (4,4)-(5,5) rank 4, S box (15,4)-(16,5) rank 5, R box (7,7)-(8,8) rank
//     6. No boxes overlap, so six groups. The four verdicts (both, order_only, space_only, neither) all read from ONE scene by choosing triples, and
//     the two relations part in KIND: the reading order always names a middle, the plane need not ------------------------------------------------

/// Mint a one-color RGBA canvas -- a local, bounded builder so this rung leans on no higher module.
fn make_canvas(garden: std.mem.Allocator, w: u32, h: u32, fill: u8) !qoi.Pixmap {
    // invariant: a canvas has real extent on both axes.
    assert(w >= 1);
    assert(h >= 1);
    const total: u64 = @as(u64, w) * @as(u64, h);
    // invariant: the canvas stays within the image module's named pixel ceiling.
    assert(total <= qoi.qoi_max_pixels);
    const pixels = try garden.alloc(u8, @as(usize, @intCast(total)) * 4);
    var i: usize = 0;
    while (i < pixels.len) : (i += 4) {
        pixels[i] = fill;
        pixels[i + 1] = fill;
        pixels[i + 2] = fill;
        pixels[i + 3] = 255;
    }
    return qoi.Pixmap{ .width = w, .height = h, .channels = 4, .colorspace = 0, .pixels = pixels };
}

/// Set one foreground pixel at (x, y).
fn set_dot(img: *qoi.Pixmap, x: u32, y: u32) void {
    // invariant: the write stays inside the buffer on both axes.
    assert(x < img.width);
    assert(y < img.height);
    const di = (@as(usize, y) * @as(usize, img.width) + @as(usize, x)) * 4;
    img.pixels[di] = 255;
    img.pixels[di + 1] = 255;
    img.pixels[di + 2] = 255;
    img.pixels[di + 3] = 255;
}

/// Paint a solid `bw` x `bh` foreground block with its top-left corner at (x, y).
fn set_block(img: *qoi.Pixmap, x: u32, y: u32, bw: u32, bh: u32) void {
    // invariant: the block stays inside the buffer on both axes.
    assert(x + bw <= img.width);
    assert(y + bh <= img.height);
    var yy: u32 = y;
    while (yy < y + bh) : (yy += 1) {
        var xx: u32 = x;
        while (xx < x + bw) : (xx += 1) set_dot(img, xx, yy);
    }
}

fn run_selftest(garden: std.mem.Allocator) !u8 {
    // --- BUILD SIX SINGLETONS on a 20x20 canvas. Every pair sits at least 2 pixels apart (8-disconnected) and no boxes overlap, so six singleton
    //     groups. The tall V is the endpoint whose reach stretches the span box down over S in the space_only shape ----------------------------------
    var scene = try make_canvas(garden, 20, 20, 0);
    set_block(&scene, 1, 1, 2, 2); // P: box (1,1)-(2,2), top 1 left 1
    set_block(&scene, 11, 1, 2, 12); // V: TALL block, box (11,1)-(12,12), top 1 left 11
    set_block(&scene, 18, 1, 2, 2); // W: box (18,1)-(19,2), top 1 left 18
    set_block(&scene, 4, 4, 2, 2); // Q: box (4,4)-(5,5), top 4 left 4
    set_block(&scene, 15, 4, 2, 2); // S: box (15,4)-(16,5), top 4 left 15
    set_block(&scene, 7, 7, 2, 2); // R: box (7,7)-(8,8), top 7 left 7

    const map = try label.label_components(garden, &scene);
    if (map.count != 6) return error.SceneNotSixComponents;
    const manifest = try scene_manifest.build_manifest(garden, &map);
    const graph = try scene_graph.build_scene_graph(garden, manifest, &map);
    const clustering = try overlap_clusters.overlap_clusters(garden, graph);
    // no boxes overlap, so each object is its own singleton group
    if (clustering.clusters != 6) return error.NotSixGroups;

    // one representative tap per object, so every read below rests on real hit-test arbiter reads
    const p_x: u32 = 1;
    const p_y: u32 = 1;
    const v_x: u32 = 11;
    const v_y: u32 = 1;
    const w_x: u32 = 18;
    const w_y: u32 = 1;
    const q_x: u32 = 4;
    const q_y: u32 = 4;
    const s_x: u32 = 15;
    const s_y: u32 = 4;
    const r_x: u32 = 7;
    const r_y: u32 = 7;

    // confirm the six page ranks the crux leans on through image/click_order.rye: P 1, V 2, W 3, Q 4, S 5, R 6 (top edge, then left edge)
    if (try click_order.click_group_min_rank(clustering, graph, &map, p_x, p_y) != 1) return error.RankPNotOne;
    if (try click_order.click_group_min_rank(clustering, graph, &map, v_x, v_y) != 2) return error.RankVNotTwo;
    if (try click_order.click_group_min_rank(clustering, graph, &map, w_x, w_y) != 3) return error.RankWNotThree;
    if (try click_order.click_group_min_rank(clustering, graph, &map, q_x, q_y) != 4) return error.RankQNotFour;
    if (try click_order.click_group_min_rank(clustering, graph, &map, s_x, s_y) != 5) return error.RankSNotFive;
    if (try click_order.click_group_min_rank(clustering, graph, &map, r_x, r_y) != 6) return error.RankRNotSix;

    // --- BOTH -- P, Q, R on the diagonal: Q reads between P (rank 1) and R (rank 6) AND Q's box sits inside the P..R span box (1,1)-(8,8) -----------
    if (try between_in_order(clustering, graph, &map, p_x, p_y, q_x, q_y, r_x, r_y) != true) return error.QNotOrderBetweenPR;
    if (try between_in_space(garden, clustering, graph, manifest, &map, p_x, p_y, q_x, q_y, r_x, r_y) != true) return error.QNotSpaceBetweenPR;
    if (try classify_betweenness(garden, clustering, graph, manifest, &map, p_x, p_y, q_x, q_y, r_x, r_y) != .both) return error.PQRNotBoth;

    // --- ORDER_ONLY -- P, V, R: V reads between P and R (rank 2 in (1,6)) yet V's tall box (11,1)-(12,12) escapes the P..R span box on x ------------
    if (try between_in_order(clustering, graph, &map, p_x, p_y, v_x, v_y, r_x, r_y) != true) return error.VNotOrderBetweenPR;
    if (try between_in_space(garden, clustering, graph, manifest, &map, p_x, p_y, v_x, v_y, r_x, r_y) != false) return error.VSpaceBetweenPR;
    if (try classify_betweenness(garden, clustering, graph, manifest, &map, p_x, p_y, v_x, v_y, r_x, r_y) != .order_only) return error.PVRNotOrderOnly;

    // --- SPACE_ONLY -- V, S, W: S's box (15,4)-(16,5) sits inside the V..W span box (11,1)-(19,12) yet S reads AFTER both (rank 5 outside (2,3)) -----
    if (try between_in_order(clustering, graph, &map, v_x, v_y, s_x, s_y, w_x, w_y) != false) return error.SOrderBetweenVW;
    if (try between_in_space(garden, clustering, graph, manifest, &map, v_x, v_y, s_x, s_y, w_x, w_y) != true) return error.SNotSpaceBetweenVW;
    if (try classify_betweenness(garden, clustering, graph, manifest, &map, v_x, v_y, s_x, s_y, w_x, w_y) != .space_only) return error.VSWNotSpaceOnly;

    // --- NEITHER -- P, R, Q: R reads after both P and Q (rank 6 outside (1,4)) and R's box escapes the P..Q span box (1,1)-(5,5) ------------------
    if (try between_in_order(clustering, graph, &map, p_x, p_y, r_x, r_y, q_x, q_y) != false) return error.ROrderBetweenPQ;
    if (try between_in_space(garden, clustering, graph, manifest, &map, p_x, p_y, r_x, r_y, q_x, q_y) != false) return error.RSpaceBetweenPQ;
    if (try classify_betweenness(garden, clustering, graph, manifest, &map, p_x, p_y, r_x, r_y, q_x, q_y) != .neither) return error.PRQNotNeither;

    // --- TOTALITY OF THE READING MIDDLE -- over the three distinct groups P, Q, R exactly ONE reads between the other two: Q does, P and R do not --
    if (try between_in_order(clustering, graph, &map, p_x, p_y, q_x, q_y, r_x, r_y) != true) return error.QNotReadingMiddle;
    if (try between_in_order(clustering, graph, &map, q_x, q_y, p_x, p_y, r_x, r_y) != false) return error.PIsReadingMiddle;
    if (try between_in_order(clustering, graph, &map, p_x, p_y, r_x, r_y, q_x, q_y) != false) return error.RIsReadingMiddle;

    // --- NO SPATIAL MIDDLE AT ALL -- over Q, R, S (an L in the plane) NONE sits inside the span box of the other two, yet the reading order still
    //     names S the middle of the three: the total order always seats a middle, the plane need not -----------------------------------------------
    if (try between_in_space(garden, clustering, graph, manifest, &map, q_x, q_y, r_x, r_y, s_x, s_y) != false) return error.RSpaceBetweenQS;
    if (try between_in_space(garden, clustering, graph, manifest, &map, q_x, q_y, s_x, s_y, r_x, r_y) != false) return error.SSpaceBetweenQR;
    if (try between_in_space(garden, clustering, graph, manifest, &map, s_x, s_y, q_x, q_y, r_x, r_y) != false) return error.QSpaceBetweenSR;
    if (try between_in_order(clustering, graph, &map, q_x, q_y, s_x, s_y, r_x, r_y) != true) return error.SNotReadingMiddleOfQRS;

    // --- SYMMETRY IN THE ENDPOINTS -- swapping the two endpoints never changes the verdict, in either register ---------------------------------
    if (try between_in_order(clustering, graph, &map, r_x, r_y, q_x, q_y, p_x, p_y) != true) return error.OrderNotEndpointSymmetric;
    if (try between_in_space(garden, clustering, graph, manifest, &map, w_x, w_y, s_x, s_y, v_x, v_y) != true) return error.SpaceNotEndpointSymmetric;

    // --- TWO ENDPOINTS IN ONE GROUP REFUSE SameGroup -- two taps on the SAME object P (its two pixels) leave no interval to be between ------------
    if (try hit_test.object_label_at(&map, 1, 1) != try hit_test.object_label_at(&map, 2, 2)) return error.PPixelsNotSameObject;
    if (classify_betweenness(garden, clustering, graph, manifest, &map, 1, 1, q_x, q_y, 2, 2)) |_| return error.SameGroupNotRefused else |err| {
        if (err != error.SameGroup) return error.WrongSameGroupError;
    }
    if (between_in_order(clustering, graph, &map, 1, 1, q_x, q_y, 2, 2)) |_| return error.OrderSameGroupNotRefused else |err| {
        if (err != error.SameGroup) return error.WrongOrderSameGroupError;
    }

    // --- A MISS REFUSES EmptyClick -- a middle tap on background (10,10) belongs to no group, so every betweenness read refuses EmptyClick ---------
    if (try hit_test.object_label_at(&map, 10, 10) != 0) return error.BackgroundNotEmpty;
    if (classify_betweenness(garden, clustering, graph, manifest, &map, p_x, p_y, 10, 10, r_x, r_y)) |_| return error.MissNotRefused else |err| {
        if (err != error.EmptyClick) return error.WrongMissError;
    }
    // a miss on an ENDPOINT refuses EmptyClick too, before the same-group test
    if (classify_betweenness(garden, clustering, graph, manifest, &map, 10, 10, q_x, q_y, r_x, r_y)) |_| return error.MissEndpointNotRefused else |err| {
        if (err != error.EmptyClick) return error.WrongMissEndpointError;
    }

    // --- A POINT OFF THE MAP REFUSES PointOutOfBounds through the arbiter, having read nothing ---------------------------------------------------
    if (classify_betweenness(garden, clustering, graph, manifest, &map, p_x, p_y, 20, 0, r_x, r_y)) |_| return error.OffMapNotRefused else |err| {
        if (err != error.PointOutOfBounds) return error.WrongOffMapError;
    }

    // --- DETERMINISM -- the same triple reads the same verdict twice ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    const c1 = try classify_betweenness(garden, clustering, graph, manifest, &map, v_x, v_y, s_x, s_y, w_x, w_y);
    const c2 = try classify_betweenness(garden, clustering, graph, manifest, &map, v_x, v_y, s_x, s_y, w_x, w_y);
    if (c1 != c2) return error.VerdictNotDeterministic;

    print("GREEN image-click-between: the THREE-CLICK BETWEENNESS relation -- turn THREE points into THREE whole-group selections (two ENDPOINT taps and one MIDDLE-candidate tap), then read whether the middle group sits BETWEEN the endpoints in the reading SEQUENCE (image/click_order.rye's click_group_min_rank) and in SPACE (image/click_bounds.rye's click_bbox), lifting the click family's ternary arity one step past image/click_nearest.rye, exact integers, NO float and NO tolerance -- the DECISIVE crux: reading-order betweenness and spatial betweenness are DIFFERENT relations that differ in KIND, a bounding-box span OVER-CLAIMS the between the total reading order names exactly, in four verdicts over six singleton objects (P box (1,1)-(2,2) rank 1, V a tall block box (11,1)-(12,12) rank 2, W box (18,1)-(19,2) rank 3, Q box (4,4)-(5,5) rank 4, S box (15,4)-(16,5) rank 5, R box (7,7)-(8,8) rank 6). BOTH -- Q reads between P and R AND its box sits inside their span box. ORDER_ONLY -- V reads between P and R yet its tall box escapes their span box on x. SPACE_ONLY -- S's box sits inside the V..W span box (the tall V stretching the box down over it) yet S reads after both endpoints. NEITHER -- R reads after both P and Q and its box escapes their span box. And the two relations part in KIND: TOTALITY OF THE READING MIDDLE -- over any three distinct groups exactly one is the reading-between (of P, Q, R only Q). NO SPATIAL MIDDLE AT ALL -- over Q, R, S (an L in the plane) none sits inside the span of the other two, yet the reading order still names S the middle, the total order always seating a middle where the plane need not. Symmetric in the endpoints; two endpoints in one group refuse SameGroup by name (no interval); a background tap refuses EmptyClick; a point off the map refuses PointOutOfBounds having read nothing; the same triple reads the same verdict twice. Compose not braid over image/click_order.rye (click_group_min_rank, click_group_id), image/click_bounds.rye (click_bbox), image/hit_test.rye (the point-to-object arbiter), and image/overlap_clusters.rye (cluster_of)\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    const garden = init.arena.allocator();
    const args = try init.minimal.args.toSlice(garden);
    if (args.len >= 2 and std.mem.eql(u8, args[1], "selftest")) {
        return run_selftest(garden);
    }
    print("usage: click_between selftest\n", .{});
    return 1;
}
